WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
Logs help administrators and analysts understand what happened on a Linux system.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:10.180
They may record authentication attempts, sudo use, service starts and stops,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.960
kernel messages, package changes, scheduled tasks, application errors, firewall decisions,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.700
and security alerts.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.640
Without logs, teams are often left guessing.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.900
With useful logs, they can build a timeline, troubleshoot failures, confirm changes,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.800
and support investigation.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.060
Authentication and sudo events are especially valuable.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.000
Failed logins, unexpected successful logins, new privileged access,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.820
repeated sudo failures,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.280
or administrative activity outside a normal change window can all deserve review.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.300
These signals do not automatically prove misuse.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.760
They help analysts ask better questions, compare activity with approved changes,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.860
and identify when a system needs closer attention.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.600
Monitoring turns logs and system signals into operational awareness.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.700
It can help detect disk pressure, service failures, unexpected process behavior,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.960
configuration drift, package changes, time synchronization problems, backup failures,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.860
and suspicious activity.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.400
Monitoring should be tuned to the system's purpose.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.740
A noisy alert that nobody owns is less useful than a small set of signals with clear

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.720
response expectations.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.700
Logs are also sensitive.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.520
They may contain usernames, IP addresses, application data, paths, errors,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.380
and details that reveal how a system works.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.240
Logs need retention, protection, and review.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.660
They should not be writable by unnecessary users, deleted too quickly, or exposed broadly.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.320
A good audit trail supports troubleshooting, accountability,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.060
and recovery because it records what changed, when it changed,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.680
and who or what performed the action.
